## Methodology

To validate the robustness of the designed amplicon scheme against the global genomic diversity of Parvovirus B19 (B19V), we performed a comprehensive *in silico* evaluation. We downloaded 503 B19V sequences with at least 70% genome coverage from the NCBI GenBank database (as of September 2026) and aligned them using MAFFT. Subsequently, an in-house Python script was utilized to dynamically evaluate all degenerate positions within the primer sequences according to IUPAC ambiguity codes. 

The algorithm performed a sliding-window search across both the sense and antisense strands of the 503 genomes to identify the optimal binding region for each primer, defined by the minimum total number of mismatches. To accurately assess the risk of amplification failure, the script calculated the overall mismatch count for each primer–genome comparison and specifically evaluated sequence conservation at the critical 3′-end (assessing the terminal 1, 3, and 5 nucleotides), as mismatches in this region severely compromise DNA polymerase extension.

## Results

Overall, the WGS-B19V primer scheme demonstrated robust compatibility with the known global genomic diversity of the virus. When evaluating the overall mismatch landscape across the 503 sequences, the primers exhibited a high degree of homology: **71.8%** of all primer-genome comparisons resulted in perfect matches (zero mismatches), and **18.5%** presented only a single-nucleotide mismatch.

Crucially, we observed that these minor sequence divergences were predominantly localized to the 5′ regions of the primers. Across all primer–genome comparisons, **95.7%** showed no mismatches within the last 3 nucleotides of the primers, with only a minimal proportion presenting one or more mismatches in this critical region. Even for primers displaying a higher frequency of overall divergence, perfect 3' end homology was maintained in over **98%** of the targets. 

This high level of conservation at the 3′ ends—which dictate proper primer annealing and polymerase extension—confirms that the overall primer mismatches do not translate into meaningful genomic dropout. These quantitative findings support the robustness and broad applicability of the designed primer scheme for B19V whole-genome amplification.
